WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
Coevolution means reciprocal evolutionary change.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.920
One species evolves in response to another.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.600
That change alters selection on its partner, which evolves in response.

4
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.620
Why does this matter?

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.720
A flower and a moth may fit each other even if only one evolved.

6
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.720
A good fit alone cannot tell us whether both partners changed.

7
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.280
We need evidence of change in both.

8
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.540
That is how we distinguish coevolution from adaptation in just one partner.

9
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.170
First, measure what each partner gains or loses.

10
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.010
Fitness means survival and reproduction.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.510
In mutualism, both gain.

12
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.410
In parasitism, the parasite gains and the host loses.

13
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.370
These are net outcomes.

14
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.970
A root fungus takes carbon and supplies nutrients.

15
00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.910
It can help a plant in poor soil and cost it in rich soil.

16
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.210
The outcome depends on conditions.

17
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.050
How can mutualisms persist?

18
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.970
Partner choice and host sanctions can help.

19
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:07.830
Sanctions reduce the fitness of partners that stop providing a service.

20
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.350
Rhizobia supply nitrogen to legumes in exchange for carbon.

21
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.910
In soybean, researchers kept the bacterial strain the same but blocked nitrogen

22
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.090
fixation with argon.

23
00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.330
The bacteria reproduced less in those nodules.

24
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.330
Changing the service revealed a host sanction.

25
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.220
For hosts and parasites, reciprocal change can take different forms.

26
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.900
In an arms race, host resistance and parasite infectivity rise through repeated

27
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.920
rounds. Escalation can slow or stop.

28
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.240
In fluctuating selection, common host types are attacked most.

29
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.640
Rare types gain, then the advantage shifts again.

30
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.760
The contrast is escalation versus a changing advantage.

31
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.750
A time-shift assay compares hosts with parasites from the past, present and future.

32
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:02.330
An arms race predicts parasites infect past hosts best and future hosts worst.

33
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:08.110
Fluctuating selection predicts a peak or dip on contemporary hosts, with no steady

34
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.110
trend.

35
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.883
Rapid evolution can also change population cycles.

36
00:02:13.743 --> 00:02:19.203
In rotifers and algae, grazing favours defended algae, but defence costs nutrient

37
00:02:19.203 --> 00:02:24.343
competitiveness. As rotifers decline, undefended clones regain ground.

38
00:02:25.523 --> 00:02:28.063
Changing algal clonal diversity changed the cycles.

39
00:02:28.883 --> 00:02:33.663
The manipulation and measured trade-off supported prey evolution driving population

40
00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:36.823
dynamics. Cycle timing alone was no proof.

41
00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:42.923
Predator evolution was not investigated, so this was not demonstrated coevolution.
